Date: Thu, 10 Jun 93 11:13:53 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: SCI-POLI: Big Science & NTI (was Rant)

Geoff Dale writes:

(my comments on nanotech and a "National Nanotechnology Initiative" elided)

> Interesting, Tim.
> 
> Nanotech may not be reachable by conventional free-market approaches,
> because there may not be any short term payoffs along the way. In other
> words, you can't make money on nano-tech without making a breakthrough.
> (Unless you count the nearly insignificant amount trickling in through
> publishing theoretical works.)
> 
> Sound familiar? It's essentially your argument. (Saturn Cafe - after the
> Frank Drake talk)

I agree, of course. But I strongly doubt a nationalized effort would
matter, for many reasons:

- not motivated to produce products, but to generate pork and
patronage

- politicalization (sp?) of goals, e.g., imagine Jeremy Rifkin running it

- national efforts are very inefficient at actually exploring the
sideways and alleyways of the "knowledge space" of a technology

(This deserves a longer treatment. For now, think of the semiconductor
industry, with hundreds of mask and chemical suppliers, thousands of
specialty services, and so on. Many large companies pursued many
"veins of ore," from silicon-on-sapphire to optically addressed
memories to I-squared L to ..... We remember the winners, of course,
but what government planner, no matter how bright, could have forecast
the success of MOS in 1965? Or forecast the microprocessor? A very
good thing the guv'ment wasn't in charge.

And the notion that the Japanese were helped by the "VLSI Initiative"
of the 1970s is misguided...I saw what happened first-hand and can
elaborate on this if anyone's really interested. Worse, for the
Japanese, was the "Fifth Generation Project," the ten-year program
that just ended a year or so ago...with a whimper. The Japs missed out
on workstations, RISC microprocessors (in fact, they missed out on all
micros, by putting their faith in the government-approved "TRON"
architecture...when was the last time you heard of "TRON"?), neural
nets, highly parallel computers (a la Thinking Machines, Intel,
MasPar, etc.), and on and on. The Fifth Generation, a model for
"industry-government cooperation" to many, at least in 1983-5, was a
boondoggle, ans actually delayed their progress.)


> Does this mean you think Nanotech *isn't* worth pursuing?
> 
> This generally bodes ill for any long term investment that doesn't have
> short term pay-offs. (This is one of the few things holding me back from
> becoming a raving libertarian, btw. But that digression might be construed
> as a basic so I'll leave that alone.)

If nanotech is worth pursuing, private industry will pursue it better.

There are two "reasons" for collective action in research:

1. The cost of capital equipment is so great that individual companies
can't afford it. This certainly does not appear to be the case with
nanotech...companies aren't pursuing nanotech, but not because there's
some incredible expense involved (at least not some _identifiable_
expense, such as for a Personal Supercollider).

2. The risks are high and the payoffs are low. Well, two ways to
handle this: insurance/idea futures/risk pools and government.
(Actually, a third way is to just "wait," to let a thousand "fools"
wander around the knowledge space until breakthroughs are found.)

Anyway, I need to cut this short, so let me close by saying I see no
evidence that there's anything in nanotech that needs government
funding.

If anyone out there can think of _anything_, other than general
platitudes like "Drexler should be supported," please comment!


-Tim May


