Date: Thu, 10 Jun 93 12:15:18 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: QM: Theories vs. Interpretations

Hmm.. seems Tim May and I mean different things by "testable".  I mean
"can be tested somehow someday", and Tim seems to mean "can be tested
soon/now".  Folks are free to mean what they want by words, but most
of the Popperian dictims regarding "testable" use my sense of the
word, I think.

I wrote:
> But let's not forget they are just rhetorical/mental tricks.  Similar
> tricks are used by most people to ignore thinking about the distant future,
> the disfunctionality afflicting many of us extropians, or to imagine
> political structures radically different from our current ones.

And Derek asked:
>Do tell, Robin!  What disfunctionality afflicts many of us?  Be blunt!

To most folks with typical values and beliefs, there's just not much
point in planning seriously for the distant future, or in considering
political alternatives unlikely to gain a winning coalition anytime
soon.  Yet these are mentally enticing activities - so mental tricks
can help folks from "wasting" time on these activities, beyond their
recreational value.  I think I take them more seriously than
recreation could justify, though maybe the rest of you don't.

Dean writes:
>One of the confusions people have about the Many-Universes
>interpretation is that they are independent universes.  

Such terms do confuse people into thinking that this approach adds a
fundamental "universe" concept to the basic QM math, and so is
incomplete without an independent (from QM math) specification of when
universes split and along what basis.  John Bell thought this.
Rather, one uses the term "universe" to refer to largely independently
evolving peices of the total state.  

These names do, however, do well connote the huge ontology inflation
one must swallow to accept the theory.  I can intellectually accept
this, but when I try to imagine it in detail, well it is a pretty
crazy theory, isn't it!  :-)  

Mike Price writes (and Fnerd wrote something similar): 
>Once a well defined fork takes place
>(corresponding often to an irreversible action) the worlds are
>completely independent of each other (according to known physics).

This is the way the story is usually told, but I think the case is
overstated.  Remember that many-worlds QM is a fully reversible
theory; the time reverse of such a fork is possible, if unlikely under
ususal thermodynamic conditions.  It doesn't really click right to say
in this case that all these universes independently became coherent
and appeared as if one universe.

Robin 

