Date: Thu, 10 Jun 93 16:56:59 EDT
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: SCI-POLI: Big Science

One of the reasons I'm against Big Science is that I think it pushes
certain developments at the expense of others, creating a kind of
distortion of development.  

In particular, concentrated funding for science and tech projects
develops just those sorts of science and tech that require further
concentrated funding to continue with.  

In other words, Big Science makes Power Tools that are accessible 
only to (or through) the powerful.  A secondary result is that
these Power Users control the direction *and interpretation* of
research.  Their control lets them put on a mass hypnosis 
spectacle about what and who is important.

Also, concentrated developments don't come with the sorts of 
correllary developments that integrate them into the way the world
works.  A paradigm of that is the atom bomb.  Some powerful guys 
took some money and smart guys and collided them in a closed place
in the desert, and then suddenly a couple cities had dissappeared 
from the earth and that's about all anyone on the outside knew.
Then the world had to spend a few decades trying to adjust.

The point I'm trying to make is not the destruction of those
cities but the discontinuity introduced, and the way the Power
Tool is accessible only to Power Users.  These sorts of things
result from most concentrated research.

So, nanotech might take a few more decades if research were not
concentrated.  And maybe I'd have to wait to be reanimated, but 
I'd much rather live in a world where science was done on a 
small, familiar scale by people like us in small groups.

Not forgetting that stopping concentrated power is no small 
trick.  Still, if it were possible to slow, say, U.S. technopork,
we'd be reducing the worst of it.

-fnerd
