Subject: PHYSICS: Question

>> They are.  Virtual photons (like all virtual particles) are not
>> constrained by the classical energy-momentum equations.  They 
>> carry (and hence transmit) as little or as much energy and 
>> momentum as they like.
> 
> So is "virtual photon" just a convenience?  What properties of 
> photons do they actually have? 

Real particles are a subset of virtual particles.  Virtual particles
have all the properties of real particles.  Feynman's book does a better
job of explaining them than I can.  A real particle is a collection of
an ill-defined number of virtual particles, all contributing, in a sum-
over-histories fashion, to produce the 'real' effect.  Nah, you're
probably better off reading the book....

Let's stick to real particles, for the nonce.

>> The energy deficit is distributed amongst an _infinite_ number of
>> soft, low frequency photons. [which answers the problem, often 
>> posed, of finding a physical infinity] 
> 
> Here's where it gets interesting.  First, if there's an infinite
> number of them, isn't it right that their frequency isn't just "low",
> but vanishingly small?  Doesn't this make their wavelength infinitely
> large? That doesn't even make sense to me.

Here's a make believe example:  
               -1/2
        N(L) = L               L = photon wavelength

where N(L) dL is the number of photons emitted between L and L+dL.
The energy emitted will go as
      /oo                /oo  -3/2           -1/2 |oo
 E = | hc/L * N(L) dL = |  hc L  dL  = - hc L  /2 |   = finite amount
     /                  /                         |
whereas photon number is:
      /oo         /oo -1/2       1/2  |oo
 N = | N(L) dL = |    L  dL  =  L  /2 |   = oo   (infinity)
     /           /                    |

This was just make-believe for clarity, but the real equations exhibit
similar divergences.  See _Quantum Field Theory_ by Itzykson and Zuber,
ISBN 00703200713, under infra-red divergence.

> Second, falling off at r^6 gets tiny, but never actually zero. 
> Doesn't this mean that ALL charged particles are exchanging an
> infinite number of virtual photons with ALL other charged particles
> in the universe?

An infinite of real photons.  Number of virtual photons is undefined.

> Doesn't this seem a bit odd?  That is, it seems like a rather
