Subject: PHYSICS: Question 

Derek Zahn asks some deceptively simple physics questions..The topic
is a real can of worms and I was really amused to watch Robin and
Mike answer by pointing to quantum field theory and some really
arcane, eclectic physics. If I were Derek, I would have been very
frustrated to find that the only way to truly understand the 
mechanisms involved in the exchange of energy between two hydrogen
ions would happen after I took about 8-10 years of physics courses!

The purpose of this post is not to answer Derek's question, because
I can't (would you believe that I did my Master's thesis in a very
related area?- I simulated several hydrogen ions trapped in a magnetic
field and computed how much vertical kinetic energy got transformed into
rotational kinetic energy when the ions got close to each other. And I
still don't understand the mechanism of how the energy was transferred.
I only knew then that Faraday's equation and energy conservation was
what I needed to use to solve the problem). I wish only to say that 
physicists have some very poor, inelegant, convoluted theories to explain 
many fundamental processes in Nature. In school, much of the physics that 
I learned was in the form of "Laws". Later I began questioning these
"Laws" and learned that some important assumptions were made that
may not have been right, yet the "Laws" are passed down from physics
student to physics student as if the foundations were rock solid.

A good example of this is Faraday's Field Concept. It conveniently 
sidesteps the problem of action-at-a-distance (i.e. how can one
electric charge repel another when there is no connecting rod between 
them?). His concept suggested that the electric field permeated
everything and everywhere, like a fluid throughout all space, so that
such actions take place locally, within the field. Maxwell then
showed that a particular combination of changing electric and 
magnetic fields could be written down mathematically in the 
form of wave equation, and that this combination would propagate 
through space at the velocity of light. This led to the postulate:
"Light consists of electromagnetic waves." 

Here are the problems.
The physical energy transported by the light waves, which propagates
at the speed of light, is taken to be energy contained in the
electromagnetic field. Once launched into space, this energy has
an independent existence, even though its source, like a star for
instance, might later explode as a supernova. So here one has an
electric field and a magnetic field, neither of which could exist
without a continuous connection to a source or a sink of "stuff", 
while their combination, the electromagnetic field, *does* have
an independent existence. 

Another problem is with Maxwell's formulation of electric and 
magnetic fields. Yes, it's a very elegant theory, but it uses
the wrong hydrodynamics to explain the nature of the waves. 
Maxwell's equations are mathematically equivalent to the behaviour 
of incompressible fluids, yet the waves in his electromagnetic
field are *transverse* waves, of a type which in the mechanical
case require a solid substance to transmit them and will not
propagate in a fluid medium. (This gives some historical background
on why scientists were so determined to find an ether.). 

I confess that I haven't read QED by Feynman yet- It's been in my
reading "IN QUEUE" for a while. I've read everything else Feynman
has written, and he's always been very good at explaining many
fundamental physics concepts. So like several other people, I
would recommend that you look at that book to answer some of your
questions.

A second recommendation for you in answering your your questions
was suggested by my sweetheart. He says that if you look at the
radiative transfer equations used by laser physicists, you might
find what you need. They are concerned with the transfer of energy
in a statistical distribution between atoms in a gas. If you can
somehow extrapolate from the statistical distribution down to two
particles, then you would have some equations to use. 


